 -SELinux:
 	-Security Enhanced Linux is a security module integrated into the Linux kernel designed to enforce access control policies that confine user programs & system services
	- It provides a mechanism for supporting access control security policies

---SELinux Modes
	-Enforcing: SELinux policy is enforced
	-Permissive: SELinux policy is not enforced but violations are logged
	-Disabled: SELinux is turned off
  -To change the Policy you need to edit the ****/etc/selinux/config****
  	SELINUX=enforcing   # Enforce SELinux policiesSELINUX=permissive  # Do not enforce policies, but log actionsSELINUX=disabled    # Disable SELinux
	Reboot the system when complete

#Commands:

---
	* sudo dnf install policycoreutils selinux-policy selinux-policy-targeted
	* sestatus
	* getenforce
	* sudo setenforce 0 --this temporarily changes the policy # 0-Permissive # 1-Enforcing
		-verify--getenforce
	* sudo chcon -t default_t /var/www/html/test.html
	* sudo sealert -a /var/www/html/test.html --where the context needs to be checked
	* sudo restorecon -v /var/www/html/test.html --this command will restore the defautl context automatically based on system policies
	* sudo chcon -t httpd_sys_content_t /var/www/html/test.html
	* ls -lZ /var/www/html/test.html --Exploring Contexts and Booleans
	* getsebool -a
	* getsebool -a | grep httpd
	* ps auxZ


